solnix: recursive /dev/poll (epoll inside epoll) -- make the wakeup of a parent pollcache lossless.

SYMPTOM (greeterclock, lixi BE solnix-32, 2026-11-03): cosmic-greeter's clock and cursor stop updating until the mouse
moves. cosmic-comp's main loop is a calloop epoll that holds the wayland server's own epoll fd, which holds every
client socket. A client request arriving on that inner epoll did not wake the compositor, which slept in
epoll_wait(-1) with the request unread. Pointer input (consms, in the OUTER epoll) woke it, it rescanned and found
the request. Every program that nests epoll fds is affected (cosmic-comp, cosmic-panel's embedded server, smallvil).

EVIDENCE: dtrace on the live compositor (pid 927): its main thread and its output thread entered DP_POLL at
14:01:25.511 and did not return for the remaining 90 s of the trace, while the greeter's last commit sat unread.
mdb -k at the same moment: the client socket's stream head had q_count 0x80 (128 bytes queued); the inner pollcache
had PC_POLLWAKE set and bit 54 (that socket) set; the outer pollcache had pc_flag 0x4 (no PC_POLLWAKE), bit 6 (the
inner epoll fd) set, and one waiter on pc_cv; the pcachelink between them was PCL_VALID. Repro
(scripts/test-poll-nested-wake.sh + .c: a socket in an epoll in an epoll, one byte written 0-1 us after the reader
re-enters epoll_wait; a lost wake = the byte unread for the whole 200 ms timeout), stock kernel: dixi 462 of 2400
nested wakes lost (19%), lixi 146 of 2400, every 300-wake run > 0; the same test with one epoll level lost 0.

MECHANISM 1 (the parent's wakeup): pollwakeup() -> pollnotify(child) sets the child's bit and PC_POLLWAKE under the child's pc_lock, then
pcache_wake_parents() did a bare cv_broadcast() on each parent's pc_cv, holding neither the parent's pc_lock nor any
lock the parent sleeps with, and setting nothing the parent can test. The parent's DP_POLL loop scans under its own
pc_lock (dppoll() of the child reports "nothing yet"), checks its own PC_POLLWAKE, then cv_timedwait()s. An event
that reaches the child after the child was scanned and before the parent is on pc_cv is dropped: the broadcast finds
no waiter and the parent's flag never changes. The leaf path (a plain fd in the pollcache) cannot lose it, because
pollnotify() runs under the same pc_lock the waiter holds until it is asleep.

THE CHANGE 1: a leaf lock pc_wake_lock and a flag pc_wakepend in pollcache_t. pcache_wake(pcp) sets pc_wakepend and
broadcasts pc_cv under pc_wake_lock. Every pc_cv waker calls it: pollnotify(), dpwrite() when fds were added, and
pcache_wake_parents() for each valid parent. DP_POLL clears pc_wakepend before each scan and, instead of sleeping on
pc_cv with pc_lock, takes pc_wake_lock, rescans if pc_wakepend is set, else drops pc_lock and sleeps on pc_cv with
pc_wake_lock, then retakes pc_lock. A parent wakeup now either shows in pc_wakepend or finds the waiter asleep.
poll(2) and ksocket_spoll() still sleep on pc_cv with pc_lock; their wakers still hold pc_lock, so they see no change.

WHY NOT TAKE THE PARENT'S pc_lock IN pcache_wake_parents(): the waker holds the CHILD's pc_lock (and the pollhead
lock, from pollwakeup()), while a parent in DP_POLL holds its own pc_lock and takes the child's in dppoll() ->
pollstate_enter(). pollstate_contend() only resolves cycles between two pollstates, not one involving a pollwakeup()
caller, so the two orders deadlock. A mutex_tryenter() would fail in exactly the window that loses the wakeup (the
parent holds pc_lock until cv_timedwait() releases it). devpoll.c's block comment already rules this out.

MECHANISM 2 (the child's scan; measured after change 1 alone still lost 4 of 320000 nested wakes on an EC2 c5.2xlarge,
0 of 160000 flat, stock 112 of 2400): a DTrace ring buffer stopped at the first DP_POLL that slept 200 ms shows the
same sequence both times. The parent's scan polls the child (dppoll -> dp_pcache_poll at ps_depth 2) -> strpoll() of
the socket -> polllock(sd_lock) finds sd_lock held, because the writer is in strrput() queueing the next byte; at
depth 2 pollunlock() refuses (POLLSTATE_ULFAIL, poll:0:pollstats:pollunlockfail, which rose by exactly the number of
lost wakes in every run), so strpoll() returns POLLNVAL without setting SR_POLLIN, and dp_pcache_poll()'s recursive
branch SKIPS the fd. strrput() then finds SR_POLLIN clear and does no pollwakeup() at all, so no waker runs and change
1 has nothing to deliver. One epoll level never loses: at depth 1 pollunlock() drops pc_lock and polllock() waits.

THE CHANGE 2: polllock() also sets POLLSTATE_ULBUSY when it refuses (the lock was only busy), and dp_pcache_poll()'s
recursive branch reports a ULBUSY child as having an event pending instead of skipping it. The caller then polls it
at depth 1, where polllock() waits for the lock and strpoll() re-arms SR_POLLIN. A VOP_POLL that refuses because it
must always drop the poll locks (prpoll() calls pollunlock() directly) sets ULFAIL only and is still skipped, so it
cannot make the parent spin. The cost is a spurious "readable" on the child epoll fd while its resource's lock is
contended, which epoll permits.

LOCK ORDER (poll.c's "Locking hierarchy" gets the same line): pc_wake_lock is a leaf. It is taken with a pc_lock
held (pollnotify(), DP_POLL before it sleeps, dpwrite()), or with a pcachelink's pcl_lock held, for the PARENT
pollcache (pcache_wake_parents()). Nothing is acquired while it is held; cv_timedwait() releases it. Lifetime: the
parent cannot be freed under pcache_wake_parents(): dpclose() of the parent takes pc_lock and every pcl_lock in
pcachelink_purge_all(), which marks the links PCL_INVALID and unlinks them, before pcache_destroy(); the waker holds
the pcl_lock while it calls pcache_wake(). DP_POLL holds a dpe reference (dpe_refcnt) while it sleeps, as before, so
its own pollcache is not destroyed while it waits on pc_wake_lock with pc_lock dropped. Dropping pc_lock across the
sleep is what poll(2) already does. A dpwrite() or a pollwakeup() may run while it is dropped; both end in
pcache_wake(), and the loop rescans after it is woken.

RESULT (scripts/test-poll-nested-wake.sh, EC2 c5.2xlarge, the same instance and nestwake binary, 8 runs each):
stock 112 of 2400 nested lost, every run > 0; change 1 only, 4 of 320000; both changes, 0 of 160000 nested and
0 of 160000 flat, while pollunlockfail rose by 5 (the busy path ran and lost nothing).

UPSTREAM: illumos-gate master at 2026-11-03 has the same pcache_wake_parents(), DP_POLL loop, polllock() and
pollunlock() (master poll.c differs from ac45d71 only by 18268, checkwfdlist; devpoll.c is identical). No tracker
issue found (searched epoll, recursive, devpoll, pollcache).

